1
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
What Chato's land doesn't kill, Chato will.

2
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Chato's Land is a western film directed by Michael Winner.

3
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:51,000
In 1870s New Mexico, a half-breed kills a bigoted sheriff in self-defense but the
posse that eventually hunts him finds itself in dangerous territory.

4
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:06,000
Pardon Chato, played by Charles Bronson.

5
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:21,000
Quincey Whitmore, played by Jack Palance.

6
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:36,000
Joshua Everette, played by James Whitmore.

7
00:43:45,000 --> 00:43:51,000
Jubal Hooker, played by Simon Oakland.

8
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:06,000
Elias Hooker, played by Ralph Waite.

9
00:56:15,000 --> 00:56:21,000
Earl Hooker, played by Richard Jordan.

10
01:02:30,000 --> 01:02:36,000
Chato's Land is a 1972 Western Technicolor film directed by Michael
Winner, starring Charles Bronson and Jack Palance.

11
01:08:45,000 --> 01:08:51,000
In Apache country, the half-native Chato shoots the local sheriff in self-defense, and finds himself hunted by a
posse of ex-Confederates, who rape his wife and leave her hogtied in the open as a bait to trap him.

12
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:06,000
After freeing her, Chato uses his superior fieldcraft skills
to lure each of the posse to their deaths.

13
01:21:15,000 --> 01:21:21,000
The film can be classified in the revisionist Western genre, which was at its height at the
time, with a dramatizing of racism and oblique referencing of the Vietnam War.

14
01:27:30,000 --> 01:27:36,000
The original screenplay was written by Gerry Wilson.

15
01:33:45,000 --> 01:33:51,000
The film explores themes of rape, self-defense, revenge, tracker, fugitive.
